{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":242643,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"a26835e3-0283-4e52-8127-daff6bb8e741","total_cost_usd":1.1378025,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":62501,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":18440,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":62501,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":18440,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":62501,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":62501},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":43189,"outputTokens":22,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.043299,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":18440,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":62501,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.0945035,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"playtester\",\n  \"verdict\": \"targets_missed\",\n  \"summary\": \"Flown, this config reaches every band and lands soft from all of them, and its parachute and full-hold-mass claims are exact — but it aerobrakes at a ballistic coefficient of 19.4 kg/m2, a twentieth of the Researcher's 387, and that one quantity makes a single pass the cheapest descent in all nine band-by-load cells, makes the return from the high band cheaper than from the suborbital band, and makes the GDD's pre-stage coarse braking pass fatal at four times heat capacity.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ablation_optimum_is_always_one_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"The ablation curve is tuned so the cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes — never a single plunge and never feathering. §4.5 risk 1 predicts this may not have a tuning at all.\",\n      \"measured\": \"One pass is cheapest in every one of the nine band-by-load cells, and ablation rises monotonically from there with no descending limb anywhere: suborbital empty 14.1 / 17.6 / 21.4 / 1,681,739% at 1 / 2 / 3 / 26 passes; low full hold 29.7 / 38.0 / 43.1 / 49.7 / 60.2 / 77.4 / 106.8 / 156.0 / 381.9%; high full hold 26.8 / 41.2 / 47.2 / 54.2 / 64.6 / 82.3 / 111.1 / 161.3 / 245.2 / 628.9 / 1739.3 / 4948.4%. The mechanism is that peak heat does not fall with extra passes — it rises (suborbital empty 72 / 75 / 76, high full hold 96 / 102 / 104 / 106), because at this beta the ship is already braking at 30-42 km inside a 43 km atmosphere and extra passes only push the braking altitude higher (34.3 km at 1 pass to 42.1 km at 26). There is nothing for the fixed thermal-cycling toll to trade against, so no value of cycle_toll_base_pct, cycle_toll_growth, heat_cost_coefficient or heat_cost_exponent can put the minimum at 2-4. The params' own balance_notes concede this; the sweep confirms it, and confirms it is a property of the flown physics rather than of the pricing model. §4.5 risk 1 is therefore realised, and its named fallback — flat per-pass ablation cost with a hard cap on passes — is the correct move. Note that skims are a different quantity and do behave: measured skim cooling reaches 0.411 of direct-entry heat from the high band, so the anti-feathering rule still has something real to price.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ballistic_coefficient_below_aerobraking_floor\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"baseline.json derives the whole drag ladder from a 1,800 kg staged ship at beta 387 kg/m2, inside the real-capsule band of 300-500 and above its own stated floor of 100, so 'the atmosphere brakes the ship rather than stopping it'.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Flown beta is 19.4 kg/m2 staged and 64.5 unstaged — a twentieth of the baseline figure and well under the ~50 floor below which the first contact with air removes the whole orbit. This is the controlling quantity behind the two findings above and below it: the ship brakes at 30,653-42,309 m against an atmosphere whose sensible top is 43,000 m, so every descent is decided in the thinnest air the model has, which is why pass count buys nothing and why cargo mass moves peak heat (96 to 105 empty-to-full at the high band, 72 to 105 at the suborbital) more than band choice does. The cause is traceable: satisfying GDD 2.3.5's full-hold doubling against a 16.1 kg/slot debris catalog forces dry_mass_kg to 90, a rocket lighter than the 90 kg astronaut flying it. The exploration grid does not rescue this — its own twelve best configs run beta 11.1-33.3, and no configuration among 5,184 scored better than 7 of 8.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"difficulty_curve_inverts_with_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"claim\": \"Reentry speed and therefore heat scale with the altitude returned from, so the value curve and the risk curve rise together; §4.5 risk 2 names the inversion of this as the exact failure the design is built against, and game_params' cost_curve prices the high band at 48.02% of plate against the suborbital band's 16.84%.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The return leg gets cheaper as the band gets more valuable. Cheapest ablation, full hold: suborbital 33.4%, low 29.7%, high 26.8%. Empty: 14.1 / 12.6 / 11.3%. Half hold: 23.3 / 20.8 / 18.7%. Peak heat moves the same wrong way — full hold 105 / 100 / 96. Touchdown speed is identical from all three bands at every load (3.00 empty, 3.72 half, 4.32 full; parachute spread_across_bands_ms = 0), so landing does not differentiate bands either. The only quantity that worsens with altitude is ascent fuel margin, 15.7 / 13.4 / 11.1%. Against the priced model this is a 2x inversion at the high band (26.8% measured versus 48.02% claimed). Cargo mass, not band, is what carries difficulty in this config: a full hold costs 2.4x the plate an empty ship costs from the same band. The grid says this is fixable rather than impossible — difficulty_rises_with_band is satisfied by 46.9% of the 5,184 configurations, and by all twelve top scorers.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"skim_multipliers_understate_measured_cooling\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier is [1, 0.93, 0.87, 0.85], and balance_notes state these were measured in the crew's own flight model at this exact config, with the high band flying [1, 0.932, 0.865, 0.799] and index 3 restated as 0.85 for a deviation of 0.051, inside the audit's 0.10 band.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The sweep flew the same experiment at fixed entry depth and got materially more cooling in every band: suborbital [1, 0.716, 0.716, 0.716], low [1, 0.662, 0.562, 0.562], high [1, 0.851, 0.670, 0.411]. The deviation at high-band index 3 is 0.388 against the claimed 0.799 and 0.439 against the shipped 0.85 — four times the audit's tolerance — and the low band misses by 0.268 at index 1. The claimed trend is also the wrong shape: balance_notes argue the measured trend is linear and does not saturate, so 0.85 was substituted to stop rewarding a fourth skim; the sweep shows the high band still improving steeply at skim 3 (0.670 to 0.411) while the suborbital and low bands saturate hard after skim 1 and 2 respectively. Consequence for the shipped pricing: feeding the measured multipliers into the params' own cost equation moves the high-band optimum from 2 skims (48.02%) to 3 or more (54.5 / 41.0 / 28.6 / 20.5% at k = 0/1/2/3), i.e. the anti-feathering guard fails at the numbers actually flown. Using the cross-band mean [1, 0.74, 0.65, 0.56] instead, the same equation with cycle_toll_growth unchanged at 1.7 gives optima of 1 / 1 / 2 skims for suborbital / low / high — the suborbital entry disagreeing with the shipped optimal_skims of 0.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"unstaged_coarse_braking_does_not_exist\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"claim\": \"The intended descent is coarse braking before staging — shallow passes with the naked hull soaking slow heat while thrust remains to reshape the periapsis between passes — with staging as the final commit. Cargo damage only begins at 100% heat, which is what is supposed to make shallow unstaged passes survivable.\",\n      \"measured\": \"A shallow unstaged pass peaks at 408 heat from the suborbital band and 385 from the low band, against reentry.heat_capacity of 100 — four times over, with survivable reported false in both. The entire pre-stage phase of the design's descent is unflyable, which also removes the between-pass burn the player's remaining thrust exists for and leaves staging as an opening move rather than a commitment. reentry.unstaged_heat_multiplier cannot reach this on its own: taking it from 3 to 1 still leaves 136 peak heat. The direction is also opposite to what baseline.json predicted — it warned the unstaged hull at beta 2,867 would brake far too weakly to be worth flying, whereas the flown unstaged beta is 64.5 and the hull brakes hard enough to cook. Across the grid this is the second-rarest target: unstaged_pass_survivable is satisfied by 15.7% of configurations, and by none of the twelve best scorers — it is the single target every 7/8 config gives up, which reads as anti-correlated with the rest of the design rather than merely untuned.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"full_hold_soft_landing_holds_but_on_a_thin_margin\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"descent_speed_full_hold_ms is 4.32, under the 5 m/s soft line with 0.68 m/s of margin, and storage tier 2 crosses it at 5.01 until parachute tier 1 brings it back to 4.34.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Confirmed exactly. Independently measured full-hold touchdown is 4.32 m/s against 4.32 claimed, delta 0, terminal velocity 4.32, and every band lands soft at every load (3.00 / 3.72 / 4.32 m/s empty / half / full). Worth knowing how narrow the ground under that is: full_hold_lands_soft is the rarest target in the exploration grid at 6.5% of 5,184 configurations, and fuel_margin_sane is the second rarest at 12.4%. This config satisfies both, which is the strongest thing the sweep says about it — but the margin is bought with a 75 m2 canopy over a 186.6 kg ship, so it scales as sqrt(mass/area) and any change to dry mass or the debris catalog moves it immediately.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"heat_index_and_full_hold_mass_claims_confirmed\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"ablation.heat_index is {suborbital 78, low 100, high 130}, derived from direct-entry peaks of 147.6 / 189.4 / 246.1 normalised on the low-band direct entry; and cargo delivers 16.1 kg per slot for a 96.6 kg full hold at 2.07x dry mass, satisfying GDD 2.3.5's doubling.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Both hold. Direct-entry peaks measured at zero skims and zero extra entry depth are 100 / 129.7 / 169.5 on the sweep's own bar; normalised on the low band that is 77.1 / 100 / 130.7 against the shipped 78 / 100 / 130 — within 1 index point in every band. Cargo measures 16.1 kg/slot, 96.6 kg full hold, ratio 2.07, matching the params exactly. All three bands are reached with fuel remaining of 62.67 / 53.70 / 44.40 kg (15.7 / 13.4 / 11.1%). Recorded because it locates the disagreement precisely: the pricing model's band spread is right, and it is the flown descent that inverts, so the fix belongs in the flight numbers rather than in the ablation table.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"proposed_changes\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.dry_mass_kg\",\n      \"current\": 90,\n      \"proposed\": 900,\n      \"reason\": \"Raises staged beta from 19.4 to roughly 401 kg/m2, inside the real-capsule band and above the ~50 floor where aerobraking exists at all, and takes unstaged beta to ~1,337 so the pre-stage coarse braking pass brakes gently instead of peaking at 408 heat; it costs a matching ~10x lift in debris masses, which the economy-balancer's own catalog_concerns already names as the honest fix for keeping GDD 2.3.5's full-hold doubling, plus the four scale-ups below.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.thrust_n\",\n      \"current\": 8000,\n      \"proposed\": 80000,\n      \"reason\": \"Holds launch thrust-to-weight at 1.81 against the ten-fold heavier ship; without it nothing leaves the pad.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.fuel_capacity_kg\",\n      \"current\": 400,\n      \"proposed\": 4000,\n      \"reason\": \"Holds the 5,423 m/s delta-v that currently buys 11-16% fuel margin at all three bands; fuel_margin_sane is satisfied by only 12.4% of the grid, so this margin is not something to spend.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.fuel_burn_kgs\",\n      \"current\": 2.5,\n      \"proposed\": 25,\n      \"reason\": \"Keeps exhaust velocity at 3,200 m/s alongside the thrust change, so the delta-v arithmetic above is unchanged rather than accidentally re-derived.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"landing.parachute_area_m2\",\n      \"current\": 75,\n      \"proposed\": 750,\n      \"reason\": \"Descent speed scales as sqrt(mass/area), so a ten-fold mass rise would take the measured 4.32 m/s to 13.66 and lose the only rare target this config currently wins; the cost is a visually enormous canopy, which is a sprite problem rather than a physics one.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier\",\n      \"current\": [1, 0.93, 0.87, 0.85],\n      \"proposed\": [1, 0.74, 0.65, 0.56],\n      \"reason\": \"Replaces values that miss the flown measurements by up to 0.439 with the cross-band mean of what the sweep actually flew; with cycle_toll_growth left at 1.7 this puts the optima at 1 / 1 / 2 skims and keeps feathering priced out, but ablation.cost_curve must be regenerated from the new array rather than left as shipped.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.optimal_skims.suborbital\",\n      \"current\": 0,\n      \"proposed\": 1,\n      \"reason\": \"Under the measured multipliers the suborbital optimum is one skim at 12.4% of plate against 16.9% for a direct entry; leaving it at 0 states an optimum the crew's own cost equation no longer produces.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.cycle_toll_growth\",\n      \"current\": 1.7,\n      \"proposed\": 1.7,\n      \"reason\": \"Explicitly proposed unchanged: the geometric toll is the only thing stopping a twentieth free skim, and re-flying after the mass change should precede any move here — but if the flown pass-count curve still has no minimum at 2-4 passes, GDD 4.5's named fallback is a flat per-pass cost with a hard pass cap, not a further nudge to this exponent.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence_notes\": [\n    \"The two experiment families in the verification sweep disagree about the crew's own config, and I could not reconcile them. The skims block reports direct-entry peak heat rising with band (100 / 129.7 / 169.5), which matches heat_index and the design. The descents block reports single-pass peak heat falling with band (105 / 100 / 96 at full hold) while the high band also brakes deeper (30,653 m against the suborbital band's 34,322 m) — deeper should be hotter. The difference is that descents lets the sim search for a braking altitude per pass count while skims holds entry depth fixed, so the descents figures are entry-depth choices as much as physics. The difficulty-inversion finding rests on the descents block and on ablation cost, which is the economically meaningful quantity, but it should be re-flown at a common entry depth before anyone tunes against it. If the sim's entry-depth search is what inverts the curve, that is a simulator bug and not a design finding.\",\n    \"The exploration grid never tested the crew's planet. Its radius axis is [800, 60000, 160000, 320000] m, gravity [3, 6, 9.81], density [0.1, 0.5, 1.2]; baseline.json's world is 200,000 m at 9.0 with density 1.5 and sits in none of those. Every satisfaction rate quoted above is therefore a statement about the neighbourhood of the design, not about this config, and the twelve top scorers all describe a 60 km / 3.0 g / 1.2 density world whose adoption would invalidate every band altitude, orbital speed and period in baseline.json. The grid also carries an 800 m radius axis point, which is not a planet anyone can fly, and its 1,296 configurations pull on every rate reported.\",\n    \"No configuration among 5,184 scored better than 7 of 8, and the grid scores no target for the 2-4 pass rule at all — it scores skimming_cools_the_entry and skim_benefit_saturates, which are about skims, not passes. My claim that the pass rule is a design problem rather than a tuning problem therefore rests on the verification sweep's mechanism (peak heat rising rather than falling with pass count, in all nine cells) and not on a grid satisfaction rate. That is weaker evidence than I would like for a blocking, design-kind finding, though the params' own balance_notes reach the same conclusion independently.\",\n    \"A cheaper single edit exists for the ballistic coefficient and I did not propose it: taking baseline.reentry.reference_area_m2 from 3.1 to 1.2 lifts staged beta to about 104 with no cascade into thrust, fuel or parachute. I left it out because it leaves a 90 kg rocket lighter than the 90 kg astronaut flying it, keeps the debris catalog that forced that number, and drives the unstaged case further out of reach rather than back into it. If the mass cascade is unaffordable this semester, it is the fallback — it buys aerobraking without buying a sane ship.\",\n    \"The simulator is a point mass with a perfect pilot. It has no attitude error, so the off_retrograde_penalty of 1 and the cosine heat rule in GDD 2.3.1 are never exercised; no debris collisions; no chute-shred check against plasma; no EVA, tether, shear or towing model at all, so nothing here tests GDD 2.3.4 or the tether-slot question in the appendix. It also cannot say whether the 0.68 m/s of soft-landing margin reads as tense or as free, whether a descent whose cheapest form is one pass feels like a decision, or whether 24-47 minute orbital periods make the skim sequence tedious. Those are Checkpoint 2 and 3 questions and a human has to fly them.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":159196,"ttft_stream_ms":2535,"time_to_request_ms":248,"type":"result","duration_ms":241001,"uuid":"fa655a26-3959-409e-adbe-43828e521260"}
